    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">What an ITIN Loan Is and How It Works</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you live and work in the United States, pay taxes, and have been told or assumed that buying a home isn't possible without a Social Security number or a green card, this article is written for you. An ITIN loan is a mortgage product specifically designed for people in your situation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Product Most Lenders Don't Talk About</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">An ITIN loan is a mortgage that uses your Individual Taxpayer Identification Number in place of a Social Security number to verify your identity and tax history. The IRS issues ITINs to individuals who have a tax filing obligation in the United States but are not eligible for a Social Security number. If you've been filing US taxes using an ITIN, you may already have the foundation for a mortgage application, even without citizenship, permanent residency, or a green card.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Gap: Conventional Lending Requires What Most Non-Citizens Don't Have</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Conventional mortgage programs backed by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac require a valid Social Security number. That requirement alone disqualifies a significant portion of the non-citizen population who live, work, and pay taxes in the US and have done so for years. ITIN loans exist outside that framework. They are Non-QM products offered by lenders who have built underwriting guidelines specifically for borrowers whose identity documentation doesn't fit the conventional mold.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Down payment requirements are generally higher than conventional loans, typically starting at 15 to 20% and sometimes more depending on the lender and the borrower's credit profile. Rates run higher than conventional rates, reflecting the Non-QM designation and the additional documentation complexity. Most programs also require at least two years of US tax filings using the ITIN before qualifying.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How ITIN Loans Are Actually Underwritten</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Without a Social Security number, the lender builds the borrower's financial profile from a different set of documents. Most ITIN loan programs require two years of tax returns filed with the ITIN, proof of current employment or self-employment income, 12 to 24 months of bank statements, and documentation of any existing credit history in the US.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Some non-citizens have established US credit through secured cards, auto loans, or other credit products over the years. Others have strong credit histories in their home countries but no US credit file at all. Lenders who specialize in ITIN loans have different approaches to thin or non-existent US credit, some accepting alternative credit documentation such as rental payment history, utility payments, and remittance records in place of a traditional credit score.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The property itself must be the borrower's primary residence for most ITIN loan programs. Investment property purchases under an ITIN loan are available through some lenders but less common and typically come with stricter requirements.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Confirm you have an active ITIN and at least two years of US tax filings using it.</strong> Those two elements are the foundation of most ITIN loan programs. If you have less than two years of filings, ask lenders whether any programs accommodate a shorter history.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull together your last two years of tax returns, 12 to 24 months of bank statements, and any documentation of US credit history.</strong> Knowing what you have before you apply gives you a clear picture of where your application is strong and where it needs support.</li>
      <li><strong>Check whether you have any established US credit.</strong> If you don't, ask your lender how they handle alternative credit documentation. Some programs will accept a history of on-time rent payments, utility bills, or other recurring obligations in place of a traditional credit report.</li>
      <li><strong>Work with a lender who has direct experience with ITIN loans.</strong> A lender who regularly closes ITIN loans knows how to structure the documentation and navigate the underwriting process in a way that a general lender does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan for a higher down payment than you might expect for a conventional loan.</strong> ITIN loan programs typically require 15 to 20% or more. Build that into your savings target before you start looking at properties.</li>
    </ol>

